# Sisu message catalog (English).
#
# Every diagnostic, label, fix, note, runtime message and command-line
# message is an entry here; code only names identifiers (D-065). The format
# is described in crates/sisu-diag/src/catalog.rs and docs/architecture.md:
#
#   CODE  KIND  IDENTIFIER  TEMPLATE
#
# Codes are stable and never reused. Ranges: E00xx lexer, E01xx parser,
# E02xx names and declarations, E03xx types, E04xx ownership, E05xx
# lowering, E06xx compile-time evaluation, E07xx capabilities, E09xx
# stage-0 limits and internal errors, E10xx packages and manifests
# (`sisu.toml`, `sisu.lock`, the module tree), W0xxx warnings, R0xxx run
# time, D0xxx driver.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- lexer
E0001 error   lex.tab-in-indentation      a tab in indentation; Sisu indents with spaces only
E0002 error   lex.inconsistent-dedent     this line's indentation matches no enclosing block; the enclosing blocks start at column {columns}
E0003 error   lex.unterminated-string     this string literal is not closed before the end of the line
-     fix     lex.close-string            close it on its line
-     fix     lex.close-bracket           close it on the line that opens it
E0004 error   lex.unterminated-block-string  this multi-line string literal is never closed
E0005 error   lex.unknown-character       `{char}` cannot appear in Sisu source outside a string or comment
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R100): a character that shows as nothing or as a space.
-     note    lex.invisible-character     it is {name}
-     fix     lex.write-space             write a space
-     fix     lex.remove-character        remove it
E0006 error   lex.unterminated-char       this character literal is not closed
E0007 error   lex.invalid-escape          `\{escape}` is not an escape sequence; write `\\` for a backslash
E0008 error   lex.unmatched-close         `{close}` closes nothing
E0009 error   lex.unclosed-bracket        `{open}` is never closed
E0010 error   lex.mismatched-bracket      `{close}` does not close the `{open}` before it
E0011 error   lex.semicolon               Sisu has no semicolons; put each statement on its own line
E0012 error   lex.invalid-number          `{text}` is not a valid number literal
E0013 error   lex.lone-close-brace        a single `}}` in a string closes nothing; write `}}}}` for a literal brace
E0014 error   lex.bad-indent-char         `{char}` in indentation; Sisu indents with ASCII spaces only
E0015 error   lex.backslash-continuation  Sisu has no backslash continuation; break the line inside brackets, after a comma or before an operator
E0016 error   lex.bad-char-literal        a character literal holds exactly one character
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R45): Python's text in single quotes.
-     fix     lex.double-quotes           text is written in double quotes
E0018 error   lex.newline-in-interpolation  an interpolation in a one-line string must end on the same line
-     label   lex.opened-here             opened here
-     help    lex.use-spaces              replace each tab with spaces

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- parser
E0100 error   parse.expected              expected {expected}, found {found}
-     fix     parse.insert-token          write the missing token
E0101 error   parse.expected-block        expected an indented block after `{keyword}` on line {line}
E0102 error   parse.block-on-same-line    a block starts on the next line, indented; nothing may follow `:` on the line that opens it
E0103 error   parse.expected-expression   expected an expression, found {found}
E0104 error   parse.expected-type         expected a type, found {found}
E0105 error   parse.expected-pattern      expected a pattern, found {found}
E0106 error   parse.expected-declaration  expected a declaration, found {found}
E0107 error   parse.top-level-statement   statements cannot appear at the top level of a module; put them in a function such as `main`
E0108 error   parse.mixed-and-or          `and` and `or` cannot be mixed without parentheses
E0109 error   parse.mixed-bitwise         `{first}` and `{second}` cannot be mixed without parentheses
E0110 error   parse.bitwise-arithmetic    `{op}` cannot be mixed with arithmetic or shifts without parentheses
E0111 error   parse.shift-arithmetic      a shift cannot be mixed with arithmetic without parentheses
E0112 error   parse.negative-power        `-x ** y` is ambiguous; write `-(x ** y)` or `(-x) ** y`
E0113 error   parse.mixed-chain           a chained comparison must point one way; `{first}` and `{second}` do not
E0114 error   parse.assignment-in-expression  assignment is a statement and has no value; to compare, write `==`
E0115 error   parse.python-conditional    `a if c else b` is not Sisu; write `if c: a else: b`
E0116 error   parse.if-expression-else    an `if` expression needs an `else` branch, because it must have a value
E0117 error   parse.trailing-tokens       unexpected {found} after the end of the statement
E0118 error   parse.arm-statement         a `case` arm on one line holds a single expression; put statements in an indented block
E0119 error   parse.chain-operator        `{op}` cannot be chained; use parentheses or `and`
# E0120 is retired: the parser takes arguments in any order, so the tree
# stays whole, and the checker reports a positional argument after a
# keyword one as E0376, with the fix that names it.
E0121 error   parse.empty-interpolation   an interpolation needs an expression between `{{` and `}}`
E0122 error   parse.closure-parameter     a closure parameter must be a name
E0123 error   parse.walrus                Sisu has no `:=`; bind with `let` or use `if let`
E0124 error   parse.keyword-as-name       `{keyword}` is a keyword and cannot be used as a name here
E0125 error   parse.unexpected-indent     this line is indented more than the line before it, which does not open a block
E0126 error   parse.expected-member       a struct body holds fields, methods and `pass`; this line is none of them
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R16; 02's departures from Python): `else` on a
# loop, and the block a statement on a `case` line needs.
E0127 error   parse.loop-else             a loop has no `else` in Sisu; keep what the loop finds in a variable and test it after the loop, as in `if let Some(i) = found:`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R25): Python's glob import.
E0128 error   parse.glob-import           Sisu has no glob imports: name what the module gives, as in `from std.collections import Deque, Heap`, or import the module and write `collections.Deque`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R53): an attribute before a statement, and the loop
# directives of 19's N-22, which are not in the language yet.
E0129 error   parse.attribute-on-statement  `@{name}` stands before a statement; an attribute stands on the line before a declaration (a function, a type, a constant, a test), and none applies to a statement
-     note    parse.loop-directive        `@tile`, `@unroll` and `@vectorize` on a loop nest are planned with the native backend (19, N-22) as checked directives, which hold or fail to compile, and are not in the language yet
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R54): another language's spelling where a parse
# error stops, written as Sisu writes it.
-     fix     parse.sisu-spelling         Sisu writes it so
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R72): habits a parse error stops at, which a note
# answers.
-     note    parse.keyword-name          `{keyword}` is a keyword, which names no binding: give it another name
-     note    parse.pattern-not           a pattern says what a value is, not what it is not: write the case it is first (`case 0:`), then the rest with `case _:`, or test it in a guard, `case n if n != 0:`
-     note    parse.generator-parens      a generator expression keeps its own parentheses, also as a call's only argument
-     note    parse.no-exceptions         Sisu has no `{keyword}`: an error is a value, a `Result` or a `T ! E`, which `?` passes on and `match`, `else` or `if let` handles
-     note    parse.go-task               a task starts in a scope, which joins it: `task.scope do s:` and `s.spawn(() -> f())`
E0130 error   parse.visibility-scope      `pub({name})` is not Sisu's: an item is `pub` to every package, or `pub(package)` to the modules of its own
-     fix     parse.visibility-package    make it visible to its package
E0131 error   parse.attribute-line        an attribute stands on a line of its own, before its declaration
-     fix     parse.attribute-new-line    put the declaration on the next line
-     fix     parse.arm-block             put it in an indented block under the `case`
-     label   parse.opened-here           this `{keyword}` opens the block
-     fix     parse.use-if-expression     write it as `if c: a else: b`
-     fix     parse.parenthesize          add parentheses
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R73): a test's, a suite's or a benchmark's name is fixed text.
E0132 error   parse.interpolated-name     the name of a test, a suite or a benchmark is fixed text, and `{{` starts no interpolation in it; a brace is written `{{{{` or `}}}}`
-     fix     parse.name-braces           write the braces as text
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R76): `else` after a statement that is no `let`.
-     note    parse.else-needs-let        `else` belongs to a `let`, `let value = f() else as e:`; a statement whose error alone is handled is `if let Err(e) = f():`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R108): the syntax 03 reserves.
-     note    parse.higher-kinded         `F[_]` is reserved for higher-kinded types, which Sisu does not have in its first version (03): a generic parameter is a type, `F`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- names and declarations
E0200 error   resolve.undefined-name      cannot find `{name}` in this scope
E0201 error   resolve.undefined-type      cannot find a type named `{name}`
E0202 error   resolve.duplicate-definition  `{name}` is defined more than once in this module
E0203 error   resolve.unknown-module      there is no module `{name}`
E0204 error   resolve.not-in-module       module `{module}` has no item `{name}`
-     note    resolve.cfg-left-out        `{name}` is left out of this build by its `@cfg({cfg})`, which does not hold
E0205 error   resolve.duplicate-field     `{name}` is declared more than once in `{owner}`
E0206 error   resolve.duplicate-variant   `{owner}` has more than one variant named `{name}`
E0207 error   resolve.duplicate-parameter  the parameter `{name}` appears more than once
E0208 error   resolve.type-argument-count  wrong number of type arguments for `{name}`: expected {expected}, found {found}
E0209 error   resolve.not-a-type          `{name}` is not a type
E0211 error   resolve.ambiguous-variant   `{name}` is a variant of more than one type ({candidates}); qualify it, as in `{example}`
E0212 error   resolve.not-an-error-set    `{name}` is not an error set, so it cannot follow `!`
E0213 error   resolve.drop-target         `Drop` can be implemented only for a struct or enum declared in this module
E0215 error   resolve.self-outside-method  `self` exists only inside a method
E0216 error   resolve.drop-signature      `Drop` has one method, `def drop(mut self)`
E0217 error   resolve.duplicate-method    `{owner}` has more than one method named `{name}`
E0218 error   resolve.self-not-first      `self` must be the first parameter
E0219 error   resolve.const-type-required  a `const` needs a type: `const {name}: T = ...`
E0220 error   resolve.import-unknown      cannot import `{name}`: it names no module of this package, of a dependency in `sisu.toml`, or of the standard library
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R119): a module beside a program of one file.
-     note    resolve.module-beside       `{name}` beside this file is a module of a package only: a package is a directory with a `sisu.toml` and the files in `src/`, which makes them one program (09)
E0221 error   resolve.field-default-order  a field with a default must not use the value of another field
-     note    resolve.default-in-function  a default is computed before the other fields have values; build the value in a function that takes `{name}` instead
E0222 error   resolve.parameter-default-order  a parameter without a default cannot follow one with a default
E0223 error   resolve.not-a-module        `{name}` is not a module
E0224 error   resolve.missing-body        `{name}` has no body; only standard-library intrinsics are declared without one
E0225 error   resolve.std-only-attribute  `@{name}` is reserved for the standard library
E0226 error   resolve.inferred-set-position  an inferred error set (`T !`) can only be the result type of a function
E0227 error   resolve.union-outside-result  `{{A, B}}` is a union of error sets and can only follow `!`
E0228 error   resolve.alias-cycle         the type alias `{name}` refers to itself
E0229 error   resolve.self-type-outside   `Self` exists only inside a type's declaration or `impl`
# E0210 and E0214 (generics and trait impls before Phase 4) are retired.
E0230 error   resolve.not-a-trait         `{name}` is not a trait
E0231 error   resolve.trait-as-type       `{name}` is a trait, not a type; a value of a type that implements it is `dyn {name}`
E0232 error   resolve.missing-trait-method  the impl of `{trait}` for `{ty}` is missing {methods}
E0233 error   resolve.not-a-trait-method  `{name}` is not a method of `{trait}`
E0234 error   resolve.method-signature    `{name}` does not have the signature `{trait}` gives it: expected `{expected}`, found `{found}`
E0235 error   resolve.overlapping-impl    `{trait}` is implemented for `{ty}` more than once; a type implements a trait at most once
E0236 error   resolve.orphan-impl         `{trait}` for `{ty}` can be implemented only in the package of the trait or of the type, or in the root package
E0237 error   resolve.missing-assoc-type  the impl of `{trait}` for `{ty}` does not say what `{name}` is; add `type {name} = ...`
E0238 error   resolve.unknown-assoc-type  `{trait}` has no associated type `{name}`
E0239 error   resolve.no-assoc-type       `{ty}` has no associated type `{name}` among its bounds
E0240 error   resolve.sealed-trait        `{trait}` is implemented only by the standard library, so `{ty}` cannot implement it
E0241 error   resolve.not-object-safe     `{trait}` cannot be used as `dyn {trait}`: its method `{method}` cannot be called through it, because {reason}
E0242 error   resolve.supertrait-missing  `{ty}` implements `{trait}`, which requires `{super}`, but `{ty}` does not implement `{super}`
E0243 error   resolve.not-derivable       `{name}` cannot be derived; `@derive` takes `Copy` and the traits that have a `comptime def derive`: `Debug`, `Default`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`, `Ord`, `Hash`, and any trait that declares one
E0244 error   resolve.derive-field        `{ty}` cannot derive `{trait}`: its field `{field}` has type `{fty}`, which does not implement `{trait}`
E0245 error   resolve.generic-default-order  a type parameter without a default cannot follow one with a default
E0246 error   resolve.impl-default        the type parameters of an `impl` or a function take no defaults; a default belongs to a type's or a trait's parameter
E0247 error   resolve.trait-cycle         `{name}` cannot be its own supertrait
E0248 error   resolve.assoc-outside-trait-impl  an associated type belongs in an impl of a trait
E0249 error   resolve.assoc-needs-type    `type {name}` in an impl needs a type: `type {name} = ...`
E0250 error   resolve.not-delegable       `{name}` cannot be delegated; `@delegate` takes `Add`, `Sub`, `Mul`, `Div`, `Rem`, `Neg`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`, `Ord`, `Hash`, `Display`, `Debug` and `Len`
E0251 error   resolve.delegate-to         `@delegate` needs `to="field"`, naming a field of `{ty}`
E0252 error   resolve.delegate-self       `{trait}` returns a `{ty}`, so `{ty}` can delegate it only when `{field}` is its one field
E0253 error   resolve.delegate-field      `{ty}` cannot delegate `{trait}` to `{field}`: its type `{fty}` does not implement `{trait}`
E0254 error   resolve.drop-generic        a `Drop` for `{ty}` must apply to every `{name}`: write `{expected}`, with the type's parameters in order and only the bounds the type declares
E0255 error   resolve.private-item        `{name}` is private to the module `{module}`; declare it `pub` to use it from another module
E0256 error   resolve.package-item        `{name}` is `pub(package)` in `{module}`, so only the modules of the package `{package}` can use it
E0257 error   resolve.not-reexported      `{name}` is imported into `{module}`, not declared there; import it from `{origin}`, or re-export it with `pub from {origin} import {name}`
E0258 error   resolve.private-field       the field `{field}` of `{ty}` is private to the module `{module}`; declare it `pub` to use it from another module
E0259 error   resolve.private-method      the method `{name}` of `{ty}` is private to the module `{module}`; declare it `pub` to call it from another module
E0260 error   resolve.not-a-dependency    cannot import `{name}`: `{package}` is not a dependency of `{this}`; add it to `[dependencies]` in that package's `sisu.toml`
E0261 error   resolve.script-main         a script's statements at the top level are its `main`, so it cannot also declare `main`
E0262 error   resolve.const-argument      `{param}` is a `const` parameter of `{name}`, so it takes a constant, such as `{name}[..., 8]`, not a type
E0263 error   resolve.type-argument       `{param}` is a type parameter of `{name}`, so it takes a type, not a constant
E0264 error   resolve.const-expression    a type's constant argument is computed where the type is written, from integers, `const` parameters, constants written that way and the operators `+ - * // % ** << >> & | ^`; this is none of them
E0265 error   resolve.const-not-int       a type's constant argument is an `int`, and this is a `{ty}`
-     label   resolve.first-impl          the other impl is here
-     fix     resolve.add-dyn             write `dyn {name}`
-     fix     resolve.add-ref-dyn         use the trait through a reference
-     note    resolve.trait-generic       a function that takes a value of any type implementing `{name}` is generic: `def f[T: {name}](x: T)`, made for each type it is called with
-     label   resolve.first-defined       first defined here
-     label   resolve.defined-here        `{name}` is defined here
-     fix     resolve.similar-name        a similar name is in scope
-     fix     resolve.similar-type        a type with a similar name is in scope
-     fix     resolve.similar-module      a module with a similar name exists
-     fix     resolve.import-from-module  import the item from its module
-     note    resolve.rename-duplicate    rename one of them, or remove the one not wanted
-     note    resolve.write-trait-method  write `{signature}` in the impl
-     note    borrow.consume-with         consume it with one of its methods that take `own self`: {methods}
E0266 error   resolve.bad-cfg             `@cfg` takes a condition: `test`, `feature="NAME"`, `not` and a condition, or `all(...)` or `any(...)` of conditions; this is none of them
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R12; 02, "Lexical structure": naming follows PEP 8
# and is enforced), and the fixes of R16 that the name pass gives.
E0267 error   resolve.not-camel-case      the name of {kind} is written in CamelCase, and `{name}` is not
E0268 error   resolve.not-snake-case      the name of {kind} is written in snake_case, and `{name}` is not
E0269 error   resolve.not-screaming-case  the name of {kind} is written in SCREAMING_CASE, and `{name}` is not
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R22): `Copy` is derived, never implemented (03).
E0270 error   resolve.copy-by-hand        `Copy` is derived, not implemented: an `impl Copy for {ty}` written by hand leaves `{ty}` moved, and `@derive(Copy)` makes it copied when every field is `Copy` (03)
-     fix     resolve.derive-copy         derive `Copy` instead
-     fix     resolve.rename              rename it
-     fix     resolve.declare-with        declare it with `{keyword}`
-     note    resolve.open-file           a program reaches files through the capability `Fs`, which `def main(sys: System)` holds as `sys.fs`: `sys.fs.read_to_string("data.txt")`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R45): Python's conversion functions.
-     note    resolve.python-conversion   Sisu converts with methods: text is read with `s.parse_int()` or `s.parse_float()`, which give an option, a number is made text with `n.to_string()` or `"{n}"`, and one number made another with `x.to_int()` or `n.to_f64()`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R37; 03, "Traits"): a trait's associated constants,
# which each impl gives.
E0271 error   resolve.not-a-trait-constant  `{name}` is not a constant of `{trait}`
E0272 error   resolve.constant-type       the constant `{name}` has the type `{found}` here, and `{trait}` declares it with the type `{expected}`
E0273 error   resolve.const-or-method     `{name}` is {what} of `{trait}`, and this impl gives it as {given}
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R68): a trait's constant where a type goes.
E0274 error   resolve.const-not-type      `{name}` is a constant of `{trait}`, which bounds `{ty}`, not a type
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R71): a dependency whose `when` leaves it out of the build.
E0275 error   resolve.dependency-excluded  cannot import `{name}`: the dependency `{package}` of `{this}` is not built for this target, which its `when` in that package's `sisu.toml` excludes
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R75): a dev-dependency is its package's tests'.
E0276 error   resolve.dev-dependency      cannot import `{name}` here: `{package}` is a dev-dependency of `{this}`, which its tests import and its code does not
-     note    resolve.dev-dependency-tests  a dev-dependency is for `test` blocks, benchmarks and the black-box tests of `tests/`: import it under `@cfg(test)`, or list it under `[dependencies]`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R76): a std module's error set written alone.
-     fix     resolve.qualify-type        name it through its module
-     note    resolve.std-type-import     `{name}` is a type of `{module}`, known where it is imported: `from {module} import {name}`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R104): the notes and fixes the manual's writers found missing, the eleventh round.
-     note    resolve.variant-of-type     `{name}` is a variant of `{ty}`, and a module's items are its types and functions: import `{ty}` and name the variant through it, `{ty}.{name}`
-     note    resolve.made-by             `{maker}` makes a `{ty}` from another module, with the private fields its module sets
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R105): coherence through the trait's type arguments.
-     note    resolve.orphan-local-type   the impl is this package's when `{ty}` is a type of this package, `{trait}` a trait of it, or one of the trait's type arguments a type of its own, as `Vector` in `impl Mul[Vector] for f64`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R106): an import on the target, and an import in a body.
-     note    resolve.cfg-import-absent   `{name}` is imported under `@cfg({cfg})`, which this build leaves out: use it inside `if comptime {cfg}:`, a branch that is built only where the import is
E0277 error   resolve.import-in-body      an import stands at the top level of a module, before its declarations, and its names are the whole module's; a function's body holds none
-     fix     resolve.move-import         move it to the top of the module
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R175; 09, "Modules"): a name the prelude keeps for
# the standard library's own code, which 09's fixed list leaves out.
E0278 error   resolve.prelude-internal    `{name}` is the prelude's own, for the standard library's code: a program sees only the names it marks `pub` (09)
-     fix     resolve.import-from-std     import it from `{module}`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- types
E0300 error   type.mismatch               expected `{expected}`, found `{found}`
E0301 error   type.integer-division       `/` divides floats; for integers use `//`, or convert to `f64` first
E0302 error   type.not-bool               a condition must be a `bool`, not `{found}`; Sisu has no truthiness
E0303 error   type.unknown-field          `{ty}` has no field `{name}`
E0304 error   type.unknown-method         `{ty}` has no method `{name}`
E0305 error   type.argument-count         wrong number of arguments to `{name}`: expected {expected}, found {found}
E0306 error   type.unknown-keyword        `{name}` has no parameter named `{arg}`
E0307 error   type.duplicate-argument     the argument `{arg}` is given more than once
E0308 error   type.missing-argument       missing the argument `{arg}` of `{name}`
E0309 error   type.not-callable           a value of type `{ty}` cannot be called
E0310 error   type.binary-operator        `{op}` cannot be applied to `{lhs}` and `{rhs}`
E0311 error   type.unary-operator         `{op}` cannot be applied to `{ty}`
E0312 error   type.assign-immutable       `{name}` is immutable; declare it with `var` to assign to it
E0313 error   type.not-a-place            this expression cannot be assigned to
E0314 error   type.non-exhaustive         this `match` does not cover {missing}
E0315 error   type.missing-return         `{name}` returns `{ty}`, but control can reach the end of its body without a `return`
E0316 error   type.return-value-in-unit   `{name}` returns nothing, so its `return` takes no value
E0317 error   type.return-needs-value     this `return` needs a value of type `{ty}`
E0318 error   type.unused-result          this `{ty}` is not used; handle it, or discard it with `_ = ...`
E0319 error   type.try-needs-fallible     `?` needs a `T ! E` or a `T?`, found `{ty}`
E0320 error   type.try-context            `?` on `{ty}` needs a function that returns `{needed}`, but this one returns `{found}`
E0321 error   type.error-not-in-set       `{from}` errors cannot leave this function, whose error set is `{to}`
E0322 error   type.break-outside-loop     `break` is only valid inside a loop
E0323 error   type.continue-outside-loop  `continue` is only valid inside a loop
E0324 error   type.cannot-infer           the type of `{name}` cannot be inferred here; add a type annotation
E0325 error   type.not-iterable           a `for` loop cannot iterate over `{ty}`
E0326 error   type.pattern-mismatch       this pattern cannot match a value of type `{ty}`
E0327 error   type.unknown-variant        `{ty}` has no variant `{name}`
E0328 error   type.pattern-field-count    wrong number of fields in the pattern for `{name}`: expected {expected}, found {found}
E0329 error   type.refutable-let          this pattern might not match; add `else:` with a block that leaves the scope
E0330 error   type.else-not-fallible      `else` after `let` needs a `T?`, a `T ! E` or a pattern that can fail; this value is `{ty}`
E0331 error   type.else-must-diverge      the block after `else:` must leave the scope with `return`, `break`, `continue` or a panic
E0333 error   type.impure-call            `{callee}` is not pure, so the pure function `{name}` cannot call it
E0334 error   type.impure-capability      the pure function `{name}` cannot use the capability `{ty}`
E0335 error   type.impure-argument        this function is passed where a pure function is required, but it calls `{callee}`, which is not pure
-     note    type.impure-path            `{function}` calls {path}
E0337 error   type.missing-field          missing the field `{field}` of `{ty}`
E0338 error   type.move-out-of-borrow     cannot move `{what}` out of a borrowed value; copy it with `.copy()`
E0339 error   type.mut-argument-missing   `{param}` is a `mut` parameter, so the call must say `mut` before the argument
E0340 error   type.mut-argument-unexpected  `{param}` is not a `mut` parameter, so the argument takes no `mut`
E0341 error   type.not-mutable            cannot modify `{what}`; it is not declared with `var`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R114): a change of what a `Shared` holds.
E0343 error   type.shared-not-mutable     cannot modify `{what}`: a `Shared` holds it, and a shared value is not changed in place, even through a `var`
E0342 error   type.it-outside-closure     `it` is the parameter of an implicit closure and exists only where a one-parameter function is expected
E0344 error   type.constant-negative-index  a constant negative index does not compile; count from the end with `^`: `{fix}`
E0345 error   type.str-not-indexable      `{ty}` cannot be indexed by position; use `.chars()`, `.bytes()` or `.byte_slice(a, b)`
E0347 error   type.bound-not-satisfied    `{ty}` does not implement `{bound}`
E0349 error   type.not-generic            `{name}` does not take type arguments
E0350 error   type.not-a-value            `{name}` is a {kind}, not a value
E0351 error   type.unknown-associated     `{ty}` has no associated function `{name}`
E0352 error   type.value-returns-borrow   `{name}` cannot be a value: it returns a borrow of its parameter `{param}`, which a function value takes by value
E0353 error   type.closure-parameter-count  wrong number of closure parameters: expected {expected}, found {found}
E0356 error   type.open-set-needs-wildcard  `{ty}` is an open error set; matching it needs a `case _` arm
E0358 error   type.let-needs-type         `let {name}` without a value needs a type annotation
E0359 error   type.no-constructor         `{ty}` cannot be constructed in code
E0360 error   type.not-indexable          a value of type `{ty}` cannot be indexed
E0361 error   type.method-needs-receiver  `{name}` is a method; call it on a value, as in `x.{name}()`
E0362 error   type.assoc-not-method       `{name}` has no `self`; call it on the type, as in `{ty}.{name}()`
E0363 error   type.tuple-index            the tuple `{ty}` has no element {index}
E0364 error   type.unpack-count           wrong number of names to unpack into: the value has size {found}, the pattern has size {expected}
E0365 error   type.if-branches            the branches of this `{keyword}` have different types: `{first}` and `{second}`
E0366 error   type.uninitialized          `{name}` is used before it is assigned
E0367 error   type.reassign-let           `{name}` is a `let` binding and is already assigned
E0368 error   type.not-ordered            `{ty}` has no order, so it cannot be compared with `{op}` or sorted
E0369 error   type.comparison-types       `{lhs}` and `{rhs}` cannot be compared
E0370 error   type.optional-chain         `?.` needs an optional value, found `{ty}`
E0371 error   type.coalesce               `??` needs an optional value on its left, found `{ty}`
E0372 error   type.float-floor-division   `//` divides integers; for floats use `/`
E0373 error   type.mixed-numbers          `{op}` cannot mix `{lhs}` and `{rhs}`; convert one of them explicitly
E0374 error   type.literal-out-of-range   `{text}` does not fit in `{ty}`
E0332 error   type.float-suffix           a float literal takes the suffix `f32` or `f64`, not `{suffix}`
E0375 error   type.type-arguments-needed  `{name}` needs a type argument, as in `{name}[T]`
E0376 error   type.positional-after-keyword  a positional argument cannot follow a keyword argument
# E0377 is retired: a call that leaves out a parameter without a default
# is E0308, with the fix that passes a hole.
E0378 error   type.main-signature         `main` must take nothing or one `System` parameter, and return nothing or `! E`
E0702 error   type.test-signature         a test takes nothing, the real `System` as its one parameter (`test "name"(sys: System):`), or fresh fakes of the capabilities it names, one of each (`test "name"(fs: Fs, clock: Clock):`)
E0379 error   type.assert-message         the message of `assert` must be a string
E0380 error   type.no-display             `{ty}` has no text rendering for `{spec}`
E0381 error   type.return-in-test         a test returns nothing
E0382 error   type.self-type-outside      `Self` exists only inside a type's declaration
E0383 error   type.from-end-outside-index  `^` counts from the end and appears only inside an index, as in `xs[^1]`
E0384 error   type.arm-needs-expression   an arm of a `match` that has a value holds one expression, not a block
E0385 error   type.bindings-in-or-pattern  `{name}` is bound in some alternatives of `|` but not in all; every alternative must bind the same names
E0386 error   type.variant-needs-fields   `{name}` has fields; write `{name}(...)`
E0387 error   type.star-outside-list      `*name` collects the rest of a list and appears only in a list pattern
E0388 error   type.rest-outside-arguments  `..` skips the remaining fields and appears only inside `Name(...)`
E0389 error   type.no-self                `self` exists only inside a method
E0390 error   type.not-a-struct-pattern   `{name}` cannot be matched with fields
E0391 error   type.const-pattern          a constant cannot be used as a pattern yet; compare with `==` in a guard
E0392 error   type.impure-value-call      `{name}` is pure, so it cannot call a function value whose type is not `pure`
E0393 error   type.impure-drop            dropping `{value}` here runs the `Drop` of `{ty}`, which is not pure, so the pure function `{name}` cannot do it
E0394 error   type.ambiguous-method       `{name}` is a method of more than one trait `{ty}` implements ({traits}); call it on the trait, as in `Trait.{name}(x)`
E0395 error   type.unsized-dyn            `{ty}` has no size of its own; hold it behind `ref` or in a `Box`
E0396 error   type.view-to-dyn            `{ty}` borrows, so it cannot become `{dyn}`, which owns its value
E0397 error   type.must-consume-argument  `{ty}` is must-consume, so it cannot be the argument of the generic parameter `{param}`, whose values the body may drop
E0398 error   type.structural-only        {callee} is hashed and compared structurally in stage 0, and `{ty}` has an impl of `{trait}` of its own; derive `Hash` and `Eq` for it, or key by a field
E0399 error   type.bad-format-spec        `{spec}` is not a format spec; it is `[[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][,|_][.precision][type]`, as in `>8.2f`, with the type one of `d x X o b e E f F % s ?`
-     note    type.method-of-traits       `{name}` is a method of {traits}
-     label   type.expected-because       expected because of this
-     label   type.found-here             this is `{ty}`
-     note    type.missing-patterns       add an arm for {missing}, or a `case _` arm
-     fix     type.use-floor-division     use `//` for integer division
-     fix     type.discard                discard it explicitly
-     fix     type.declare-var            declare `{name}` with `var`
-     fix     type.from-end-index         count from the end with `^`
-     fix     type.add-error-set          add `{set}` to the function's error set
-     fix     type.add-copy               copy it
-     fix     type.call-with-value        the function takes a value where a reference is passed: call it from a closure that passes the value
-     fix     type.convert-to-float       convert the `int`
-     note    resolve.cfg-target          a body tests the target with `if comptime {condition}:`, whose other branch is left out of the build
-     note    type.pure-adapter           `{callee}` takes any function, which its iterator calls, so it is not pure, whatever the function given: a `pure` function folds the items with a `pure` function, `fold(0, (total, x) -> total + f(x))`, or loops over them
-     note    resolve.const-as-method     a name in capitals is a constant's: a trait's constant is given as `const {name}: T = ...`
-     fix     type.derive-trait-for       derive it for `{ty}`
-     note    type.operator-trait-left    `{op}` with `{ty}` on the left is the method of `{trait}` that `{ty}` implements: an `impl {trait}[{rhs}] for {ty}` gives it, which the package that declares `{rhs}` may write; or write the `{rhs}` first
-     note    type.bigint-literal         a larger integer is a `BigInt` (`from std.num import BigInt`): `BigInt.parse("{text}")`, or the literal beside a `BigInt`, which it becomes
-     fix     type.lend-it                lend it
-     fix     type.convert-to-int         make the `{ty}` an `int`, which holds the values of both
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R45): a value where an option of it is expected.
-     fix     type.wrap-some              make it the option's value with `Some`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R113): an optional reference compared with an
# optional value.
-     fix     type.copy-optional          make the optional reference an optional value to compare them
-     note    type.operator-trait         `{op}` on a `{ty}` is the method of the trait `{trait}`: an `impl {trait}[{rhs}] for {ty}` gives it one
-     note    type.keyword-only           `{param}` and the parameters after it are keyword-only: pass them by name, `{param}=...`
-     fix     type.result-or              take the result's value, or the default when it is an error, with `or`
-     note    type.question-dot           `?.` is one token, the optional chain; to pass the error on first and then call, write `(x?).f()` or bind the value with `let` first
-     fix     type.change-copy            or change a copy of `{name}` that the body declares, which leaves the callers' value alone
-     note    type.shared-changes         what a `Shared` holds is shared, so it is not changed in place: put the part that changes in a `Cell` or a `Mutex` inside it, and change it through that
-     fix     type.take-out               or take it out of the list, which then no longer holds it
-     note    type.capture-borrow         a plain closure borrows `{name}` instead, while it lives; a copy made first is no more `Copy` than `{name}` is
-     fix     type.interpolate            write the value into the text
-     note    type.option-not-value       the value may be missing: give a default with `??`, or take it apart with `if let` or `match`
-     note    type.read-guard-value       a read guard only reads the value; `write()` gives the guard that changes it
-     fix     type.similar-member         the type has a member with a similar name
-     fix     type.take-mut               take `{name}` with `mut`
-     fix     type.remove-pure            remove `pure`
-     fix     type.show-debug             show its `Debug` form
-     fix     type.add-bound              bound `{param}` by `{bound}`
-     fix     type.add-hole               pass `{name}` as a hole to fill
-     fix     type.unquote                write the number without quotes
-     fix     type.call-conversion        call `{method}`, which gives the type expected
-     fix     type.name-argument          name it `{name}`
-     fix     type.drop-return-value      return without the value
-     note    type.spec-letters           `d`, `x`, `X`, `o` and `b` format integers, `e`, `E`, `f`, `F` and `%` format numbers, and text takes none of them
-     note    type.break-use-return       `return` leaves the function; `break` leaves only a loop
-     note    type.int-range              `{ty}` holds {min} to {max}
-     note    type.float-range            `{ty}` holds magnitudes up to {max}
-     note    type.missing-return-end     return a value on that path too, or end the body with a `return`
-     note    type.main-forms             write `def main():` or `def main(sys: System):`, returning nothing or `!`
-     fix     type.add-mut                write `mut` before the argument
-     fix     type.remove-mut             remove `mut`
-     fix     type.add-impl               `{ty}` has every method of `{trait}`; add `impl {trait} for {ty}`
-     help    type.truthiness             write the test explicitly, as in `not xs.is_empty()` or `x != 0`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R10 and R16): every missing pattern, and the fixes
# the manual's writers found missing.
-     note    type.missing-patterns-each  add an arm for each of {missing}, or a `case _` arm
-     note    type.more-missing           these are the first {count} patterns no arm covers, and there are more
-     fix     type.derive-trait           derive it
-     note    type.borrowed-items         the items of a borrowed `{ty}` are references, which are `{item}` values only when `{item}` is `Copy`
-     fix     type.make-path              make a `Path` of the text
-     fix     type.return-errors          let `{name}` return its errors
-     fix     type.iterate-chars          iterate over its characters
-     fix     type.iterate-bytes          iterate over its bytes
-     fix     type.char-as-text           write the character as text
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R24): the fixes the manual's later chapters found
# missing.
-     fix     type.join-items             join the items with their own method
-     fix     type.sisu-spelling          Sisu spells it this way
-     fix     type.iterate-first          iterate it first
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R25).
-     note    type.annotate-params        a closure's parameters take their types from where the closure is passed; one kept in a `let` names them, as in `(a: int, b: int) -> a + b`
-     note    type.sort-by-key            Sisu takes no `key=`: sort a list by a key with `sort_by`, as in `xs.sort_by((a, b) -> len(a).cmp(len(b)))`, and pick an item by one with an iterator's `max_by` or `min_by`, as in `xs.iter().max_by(len(it))`
-     note    type.enumerate-start        `enumerate` counts from 0 and takes no `start`: add the start where the count is used, as `i + 1`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R27; D-061, 10 and 17): a release build rejects
# typed holes, so that blocked is never a shipped state.
E0445 error   type.hole-in-release        a release build rejects the typed hole {hole} of type `{ty}`: blocked is never a shipped state
-     note    type.hole-placeholder       fill the hole before the release build; a placeholder meant to ship is `panic("...")`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R28; 06, "Effects"): what observes the schedule.
-     note    type.observes-schedule      an `Atomic` is shared by tasks, so what a read of it gives depends on the schedule, which a `pure` function's result may not (06)
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R33; D-076): a `Decimal` quotient is rounded where
# it is made.
-     note    type.decimal-quotient       a quotient of `Decimal`s has as many digits as it is given: `a.div(b, places, mode)` rounds it to `places` digits after the point by `mode` (`Rounding.HalfEven`, ...), and `a.to_ratio()` makes the exact fraction
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R33; 03): what `@float` takes.
E0448 error   resolve.float-attribute     `@float` takes `fast`, which allows contraction and reassociation in the function (03); floating point is strict elsewhere
E0446 error   type.par-body-impure        `{callee}` is not `pure`, so the body of a `par` loop cannot call it: a `par` body runs on a worker and is `pure` (06)
E0447 error   type.par-body-changes       the body of a `par` loop changes nothing but its own values, and `{name}` is declared before the loop: compute each value with `par.map` and combine them with `sum` or a reducer (06)
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R37; 03, "Traits"): a trait's constant is each
# implementing type's.
E0449 error   type.trait-constant         each impl of `{trait}` gives its own `{name}`, so it is read through a type: `T.{name}` for a type `T` that implements `{trait}`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R74, R76 and R77): the notes and fixes the manual's writers found missing.
-     note    type.hidden-module          `{name}` here is a local, which hides the module `{module}` that this module imports: give the local another name
-     note    type.positional-only        `{arg}` is a positional-only parameter, which is passed without its name
-     fix     type.drop-name              pass it by position
-     fix     type.get-default            `get` gives an option, whose default `??` gives
-     note    type.instance-code          in the instance of `{name}` that this call makes: `{code}`
-     note    type.instance-owns          what the instance gives on is borrowed there: a template's parameter that it gives on, as to `reflect.construct`, is taken `own`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R131; X7): the template's line that wrote the
# instance's.
-     label   type.instance-line          the template's line that wrote it
-     note    type.compare-result         a `T ! E` holds a value or an error: compare it with `Ok(...)` or `Err(...)`, or ask `.is_ok()`; in `ensures`, `result` is the whole result of a function that returns one
-     note    type.it-argument-only       `it` makes a closure of a call's argument only; elsewhere, as for an item of a list of functions, write the closure with its parameter: `x -> ...`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R104): the notes and fixes the manual's writers found missing, the eleventh round.
-     note    type.item-stays-in-list     `{list}` owns its list, which keeps the item `{what}` reads and only lends it: copy the item, or take it out of the list
-     note    type.narrow-capability      a `{found}` is a `{cap}`, which a parameter `{field}: {cap}` takes where a whole `System` is taken now; a caller that has a `System` passes `sys.{field}`
-     fix     type.float-division         divide them as floats
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R108): a float's total order.
-     note    type.total-order            an `f64` is ordered only partially, a NaN with nothing: `x.total_order()` gives the total order, as in `sorted(xs.iter().map(x -> x.total_order()))`, and `max_by` and `min_by` take the `f64` itself

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ownership
# The signature rules (E0411-E0415) are the resolver's; the rest are the
# ownership checker's (sisu-borrowck), which runs on SIR.
E0400 error   borrow.use-after-move       `{name}` was moved on line {line} and cannot be used afterwards
E0401 error   borrow.use-after-partial-move  `{name}` cannot be used as a whole, because `{part}` was moved out of it on line {line}
E0402 error   borrow.modify-while-borrowed  `{name}` is borrowed on line {line} and cannot be modified until that borrow ends on line {end}
E0403 error   borrow.use-while-mut-borrowed  `{name}` is borrowed with `mut` on line {line} and cannot be used until that borrow ends on line {end}
E0404 error   borrow.move-while-borrowed  `{name}` is borrowed on line {line} and cannot be moved until that borrow ends on line {end}
E0405 error   borrow.mut-while-borrowed   `{name}` is already borrowed on line {line}, so it cannot be borrowed with `mut` until that borrow ends on line {end}
E0406 error   borrow.does-not-live-long-enough  `{name}` does not live long enough: it is borrowed on line {line}, and the borrow is still used on line {end}, after `{name}` is gone
E0407 error   borrow.return-local         this returns a borrow of `{name}`, which belongs to `{function}` and is gone when it returns
E0408 error   borrow.return-not-from      this returns a borrow of the parameter `{param}`, which is not in the `from` clause of `{function}`
E0409 error   borrow.store-in-plain-type  `{ty}` cannot hold this value, because it borrows `{name}`; only a `view struct` holds borrows
E0410 error   borrow.store-into-caller    this value borrows `{name}`, so it cannot be stored in `{param}`, which belongs to the caller of `{function}`
E0411 error   borrow.view-field-in-plain-struct  `{ty}` holds `{field}: {fty}`, which borrows; only a `view struct` may hold a borrow
E0412 error   borrow.view-in-enum         `{ty}` cannot hold `{fty}`, which borrows; an enum has no view form, so store an owned value such as `String`
E0413 error   borrow.from-required        `{name}` returns `{ret}`, which borrows, and has more than one borrowed parameter ({params}); name the ones it borrows from with `from`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R16): a text field owns its text.
-     fix     borrow.own-string           own the text instead
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R24): a result of text owns it.
-     fix     borrow.own-result           return owned text instead
-     note    borrow.return-text          a `str` views text, and a copy of it is the same view: return a `String`, which owns its text (`-> String`, or `list[String]` for views in a list)
E0414 error   borrow.from-not-a-parameter  `{name}` in the `from` clause is not a borrowed parameter of `{function}`
E0415 error   borrow.from-without-borrow  `{function}` returns `{ty}`, which does not borrow, so it takes no `from` clause
E0416 error   borrow.move-out-of-drop     cannot move `{part}` out of `{name}`, because `{ty}` implements `Drop`, which needs the whole value
E0417 error   borrow.must-consume         `{name}` is never consumed: a value of `{ty}` must be passed to an `own` parameter before it goes out of scope
E0418 error   borrow.must-consume-exit    this leaves the scope with `{name}` unconsumed: a value of `{ty}` must be passed to an `own` parameter first
E0419 error   borrow.move-out-of-place    cannot move out of `{name}` through a reference or an index; copy it with `.copy()`
E0420 error   borrow.must-consume-intrinsic  `{callee}` may drop values that `{name}` holds, and a value of `{ty}` is must-consume: its values must be passed to an `own` parameter
E0421 error   borrow.view-in-owner        `{ty}` borrows, so it cannot be put in `{owner}`, which can outlive what it borrows (04, rule 3)
E0422 error   borrow.must-consume-in-owner  `{ty}` is must-consume, so it cannot be put in `{owner}`, which can drop it where the checker does not see
E0423 error   type.own-closure-capture    an `own` closure keeps a copy of `{name}`, and `{ty}` is not `Copy`; stage 0 copies captures (a capture by move arrives with tasks)
E0424 error   type.own-closure-changes    an `own` closure keeps a copy of `{name}` and cannot change it; borrow it with a plain closure instead
E0425 error   task.closure-literal        `{function}` takes a closure written at the call (or a `do` block), so that what it captures can be checked; a function value is not one
E0426 error   task.not-send               `{name}` moves into the task, but `{ty}` is not `Send`
E0427 error   task.not-sync               `{name}` is shared with the task by reference, but `{ty}` is not `Sync`
E0428 error   task.borrows-body-local     the task borrows `{name}`, which belongs to the scope's body: the body ends before the scope joins its tasks
E0429 error   task.borrows-elsewhere      the task borrows `{name}`, but only a task spawned on a scope in that scope's body may borrow, and only what the body's environment borrows
E0430 error   task.scope-escapes          a scope's body cannot return `{ty}`: a scope and the handles of its tasks end with the scope
E0431 error   task.race-types             the tasks of a race return one type: this one returns `{found}`, an earlier one `{expected}`
E0432 error   task.result-unknown         the type of the task's result must be known where it is joined
E0433 error   task.result-not-send        the task returns `{ty}`, which is not `Send`, so it cannot cross back to the task that joins it
E0434 error   task.select-arm             a `select` arm waits for a `recv`, `send` or `join` call, a `Timer` or a `Cancellation`, not `{ty}`
E0437 error   task.not-send-mut           `{name}` is changed by the task through a reference, but `{ty}` is not `Send`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R21): a collection's items are values it owns, or
# views, and never references (04, rule 3).
E0438 error   borrow.ref-in-collection    `{ty}` would hold references, and a collection holds values it owns or views, never references (04, rule 3)
-     fix     borrow.own-items            hold the values themselves: `{ty}`
-     fix     borrow.copy-items           collect copies of the items
-     note    borrow.items-owned          put copies in it, made with `.copy()` (or `.to_string()` for text), or views, such as `str` for a `String`; a reference is kept only in a `view struct`'s `ref` field
E0435 error   type.yield-outside-generator  `yield` belongs in the body of a generator, a function that returns `Iter[T]` (not in a closure)
E0436 error   type.generator-result       `{name}` yields, so it must return `Iter[T]`, not `{ty}`
-     help    task.wrap-mutex             wrap it: `Mutex[{ty}]` shares mutation between tasks; or move the value with an `own` task
-     help    task.use-own                move it into the task instead: write `own () -> ...` (or `do own:`), or copy what the task needs into a local before the `spawn`
-     help    task.make-outside           make `{name}` before `task.scope`, outside the scope's body, so that it outlives every task; `own () -> ...` (or `do own:`) would move it into the first task and leave none for the next
-     help    task.shared-change          each task the loop spawns changes `{name}`, and the tasks run at once: keep it in a `Mutex` (`Mutex(0)`, changed through `{name}.lock().value`), or have each task return its part and combine them after the scope
-     note    task.field-path             because of `{path}`, a `{ty}`
-     note    task.make-inside            make the value inside the task, or give the task a value that is `Send` to make it from
-     note    task.return-send            return a value that is `Send` from the task, and make the rest from it after the join
-     note    task.join-inside            join the task inside the scope and return what it returned
-     note    task.write-closure          write the closure at the call, one that calls the function: `() -> work()`
-     label   borrow.moved-here           `{name}` is moved here
-     label   borrow.moved-in-loop        `{name}` is moved here, in an earlier iteration of the loop
-     label   borrow.borrowed-by          borrowed here by `{borrower}`
-     label   borrow.borrowed-here        borrowed here
-     label   borrow.later-used           the borrow is still used here
-     label   borrow.declared-here        `{name}` is declared here
-     label   borrow.assigned-here        `{name}` is assigned here first
-     note    borrow.move-after           move this after line {end}, where the borrow ends, or work on a copy made with `.copy()`
-     note    borrow.copy-before-move     copy it with `.copy()` before it is moved, or move it later
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R24): each remedy with its cost.
-     note    borrow.move-remedies        copy it with `.copy()` before the move, which makes a second value (for a list or a `String`, an allocation); move it later, after its last use, which costs nothing; or, where what it moves into only reads it, take it there without `own`, and the call lends it
-     note    borrow.use-own-closure      a closure that is stored or returned must own what it captures: write it as an `own` closure
-     note    borrow.return-owned         return an owned value instead, such as a copy made with `.copy()`
-     note    borrow.unvisited-rest       the loop that consumes `{name}` has not visited every element yet; finish the loop first, or return what is left
-     note    borrow.declare-outside      declare `{name}` where it lives as long as the borrow is used, outside the block or loop it is in now
-     note    borrow.bind-temporary       a temporary lives until the end of its statement; bind it with `let` first so it lives as long as the borrow
-     note    borrow.from-only-parameters  a `from` clause names only borrowed parameters the result comes from: remove this name, or add the parameter
-     note    borrow.copy-part            copy the part with `.copy()`, which leaves the value whole for its `Drop`
-     note    borrow.consume-first        consume the value before it would go in, or keep it in a variable until it is consumed
-     note    borrow.consume-each         take the values out first and consume each of them: `for x in own xs`
-     note    borrow.consume-before-call  consume the value before the call, or call a function written for its type
-     note    borrow.own-instead          put an owned value in it instead, such as a `String` made with `.to_string()`
-     note    borrow.store-owned          store an owned value instead, such as a copy made with `.copy()` or `.to_string()`
-     note    borrow.owned-field          declare the field with an owned type, such as `String` for `str`
-     note    type.call-instead           call it where its result is needed, or pass a closure that returns an owned copy of the result
-     note    type.capture-copy           copy it into a local first (`let c = name.copy()`) and capture that, or use a plain closure, which borrows it
-     note    type.plain-closure          use a plain closure, which borrows the variable and may change it
-     note    type.borrow-with-mut        borrow it with `ref mut` to change what the borrow refers to
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R55): a `var` that holds a borrow, a borrowed
# parameter given where its value is moved, an `int` where a sized integer
# is expected, and a capitalised name a `let` binds.
-     note    type.var-holds-borrow       `{name}` is a `var`, but it holds `ref {ty}`, a borrow of what it was given, which changes nothing of its own; a copy is its own to change
-     note    type.borrowed-to-own        {callee} takes `{param}` with `own`, which moves the value in, and `{name}` is a borrowed parameter here, which only lends its value
-     fix     type.take-own               take `{name}` with `own`, so that it is this function's to give on
-     note    type.narrow-int             an `int` narrows to `{ty}` through `.to_{ty}()`, which gives an option: `None` when the value does not fit
-     fix     type.lowercase-binding      a binding's name starts with a lowercase letter
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R50): a trait's constant read through a type
# parameter where a type's constant argument goes.
-     note    resolve.const-through-param  `{name}` is a trait's constant, which each impl gives: through a type parameter it is a value of the instance, known where the function runs, and a type's constant argument is known where the type is written; take it as a `const` parameter, `[T: Shape, const N: int]`, which a caller gives as `Square.{name}`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R70): a derivable trait of a std module that is not
# imported.
-     note    resolve.derive-import       `{name}` is a trait of `{module}`, whose derive is known where it is imported: `from {module} import {name}`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R70): where in a derived impl a diagnostic placed at
# its derive is.
-     note    type.derived-code           in the impl of `{trait}` that the derive writes for `{ty}`: `{code}`
-     note    type.assign-every-path      give it a value on every path before this use, or where it is declared
-     note    type.dyn-owned              make the `dyn` from a value that owns what it holds, or keep the view as its own type
-     label   borrow.borrowed-param       `{name}` is a borrowed parameter
-     label   borrow.not-borrowed-param   `{name}` lends nothing: it is passed with `own`, or its value is `Copy` and cannot borrow
-     label   borrow.no-such-param        `{function}` has no parameter `{name}`
-     label   borrow.result-cannot-borrow  `{ty}` cannot hold a borrow
-     label   borrow.type-declared-here   `{ty}` is declared here
-     label   borrow.drop-impl-here       `{ty}` implements `Drop` here
-     label   borrow.assigned-in-loop     `{name}` is assigned here, in an earlier iteration of the loop
-     label   borrow.made-here            the value is made here
-     label   type.declared-pure-here     `{name}` is declared `pure` here
-     fix     borrow.declare-view         declare it a view
-     fix     borrow.remove-from          remove the `from` clause
-     fix     borrow.add-from             name `{param}` in the `from` clause
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R77): where the borrow of a loop or of `sorted` ends.
-     note    borrow.loop-ends            the borrow lasts through the loop, which ends after line {end}: change `{name}` after the loop, or loop over a copy made with `.copy()`
-     note    borrow.sorted-refs          `sorted` gives references to items that are not `Copy`, which a list does not hold: sort a list of its own in place instead, `xs.sort()`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R104): a lent capability, and a binding a loop makes anew.
-     note    borrow.lent-capability      `{ty}` lasts for the call it is lent to, so nothing keeps it: keep the `{cap}` itself, taken as a parameter of type `{cap}`, or do the work inside the call
-     note    borrow.bound-anew           `{name}` is bound anew at each step of the loop, while a borrow of the one before is still used on line {end}: keep a copy of what is borrowed, made with `.copy()`, in a value that owns it, such as a `String`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- lowering and SIR
E0500 error   sir.parse                   SIR: {detail}
E0501 error   sir.invalid                 invalid SIR in `{function}`: {detail}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- compile-time evaluation
E0600 error   comptime.not-constant       `comptime` evaluates what is known at compile time, and `{name}` is known only at run time
E0601 error   comptime.io                 the compile-time evaluation of this expression used {what}; nothing reaches the world at compile time
E0602 error   comptime.panicked           the compile-time evaluation of this expression panicked: {message}
E0603 error   comptime.budget             the compile-time evaluation in `{item}` used up its budget of {steps} steps; raise it with `@comptime_budget(steps=N)` on `{item}`
E0604 error   comptime.not-kept           {what} made at compile time cannot be kept for run time; numbers, text, booleans, characters, and lists, sets, dicts, tuples, options, results, structs and enums of them can
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R160): what a compile-time result keeps of functions.
-     note    comptime.kept-functions     a function is kept as the function it names, and a closure as that closure when it captures nothing or only numbers, text, booleans and characters; a closure that captures a binding would reach the compile-time code's bindings, which run time does not have
E0605 error   comptime.only               `{name}` is a `comptime def`: it can be called only at compile time, inside `comptime`
E0606 error   comptime.budget-attribute   `@comptime_budget` takes `steps=N`, a positive number of steps
E0607 error   comptime.derive-template    a trait's derive is `comptime def derive(T: type) -> impl {trait} for T`, whose body is `return impl {trait} for T:` with the impl's methods
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R130; 08, G21): what may come before the `return impl`.
-     note    comptime.derive-checks      only checks of the type may come before the `return impl`: `comptime assert`, and `comptime panic` and `comptime if` of them, which write no code
E0608 error   comptime.derive-failed      deriving `{trait}` for `{ty}` stopped at compile time: {reason}
-     label   comptime.derive-here        the derive's template, where it stopped
E0609 error   comptime.instance-failed    making `{name}` stopped at compile time: {reason}
-     label   comptime.instance-here      the template, where it stopped
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R34; 08): a `for` over what compile time knows.
-     fix     comptime.write-comptime-for  unroll the loop at compile time with `comptime for`
E0610 error   comptime.instances-unbounded  `{name}` needs more than {count} instances: its instances call it with ever more types
E0611 error   comptime.template-value     `{name}` reflects on its type parameters, so it is made for each call's types and cannot be used as a value
E0612 error   comptime.embed-failed       cannot embed `{path}`: {reason}
E0613 error   comptime.embed-path         `embed` takes the path of a file as a string literal
E0614 error   comptime.no-fake            `cap.Fake[{name}]` needs a trait of the program whose methods can all be forwarded: a `self` or `mut self` receiver, no type parameters, and no borrow or `Self` in a signature
# Stage 1's (proposal/22-unsafe.md, D-085): code that compile-time
# evaluation writes holds no `unsafe`.
E0615 error   comptime.unsafe-written     code that compile-time evaluation writes holds no `unsafe`, so that every unsafe block stays in a source file, where `sisu audit --unsafe` shows it
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R159): `E0609` of a function that takes no type
# parameters, which is made once and is no instance, in a plain function's
# words.
E0616 error   comptime.function-failed    the compile-time code of `{name}` stopped: {reason}
-     label   comptime.function-here      the compile-time code, where it stopped
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R165): a configuration module's constant that
# `eval`'s form has no way to write.
E0617 error   eval.no-form                `{name}` holds {what}, which {form} cannot write

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- capabilities
E0700 error   cap.not-shareable          `{ty}` cannot become `{cap}`: a capability may reach every task, and `{ty}` is not `{auto}`
E0701 error   cap.internal               `{name}` is internal to the standard library; a program reaches the world only through the capabilities it is given
E0703 error   cap.untrusted              the package `{package}` is not trusted with {what}; a dependency may contain `unsafe` and `extern` only when `[trust] unsafe` in the root package's `sisu.toml` lists it

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contracts and refinement types (07)
E0800 error   contract.misplaced          `{keyword}` is a function's contract and belongs at the start of its body, before its first statement
E0801 error   contract.not-pure           a contract only reads what it is given, and `{callee}` is not `pure`
-     fix     contract.make-pure          declare it `pure`, if it only reads what it is given
E0802 error   contract.changes            a contract only reads what it is given, and this changes it
E0803 error   contract.old-outside        `old(...)` is a value as it was on entry, which only an `ensures` clause can ask for
E0804 error   contract.old-arguments      `old` takes one expression, as in `old(self.balance)`
E0805 error   contract.generator-ensures  a generator has no `ensures`: what it returns is its iterator
E0806 error   contract.invariant-field    `{what}` is part of a value of `{ty}`, whose invariant only its own methods may change and check again; change it through one of them
E0807 error   contract.not-in-test        a test has no contract; check what it needs with `assert`
E0810 error   refine.not-satisfied        `{value}` is not of type `{ty}`: `{predicate}` is false for it
E0811 error   refine.needs-try-from       expected `{expected}`, found `{found}`; a value becomes a `{expected}` where its predicate is checked, with `{name}.try_from(...)`
E0812 error   refine.change-through-base  `{what}` is of type `{ty}`, which a change through its base could take outside `{predicate}`; change a copy of the base and check it again with `try_from`
E0813 error   refine.own-impl             `{ty}` refines `{base}` and has its reading impls; it cannot have an impl of `{trait}` of its own
E0814 error   refine.predicate-type       the predicate of `{ty}` must be a `bool`, found `{found}`
-     help    refine.use-try-from         check it where it runs: `{name}.try_from(...)`
-     note    refine.copy-as-base         a copy of `{name}` is a `{ty}` too: change a copy typed as the base, `var copy: {base} = {name}.copy()`, and check it again with `{ty}.try_from(copy)`
E0820 error   test.no-generator           `{ty}` has no generator for property tests: {why}
E0821 error   test.refines-mismatch       `@refines({reference})` on `{name}` cannot be checked: {why}
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R104): the methods that change a value with an invariant.
-     note    contract.invariant-methods  the methods of `{ty}` that take `mut self` change it and check the invariant again: {methods}
-     note    contract.invariant-method   give `{ty}` a method that makes the change, `def NAME(mut self, ...)`, which checks the invariant when it returns, and call it

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- stage-0 limits and internal errors
E0900 error   stage0.unsupported          {construct} is not implemented in stage 0 yet; it arrives with {phase}
E0901 error   stage0.internal             internal compiler error in {stage}: {detail}
E0971 error   sim.unmodelled-extern       this `sim` build reaches `{callee}`, an `extern` call with no simulation model, in `{function}` at {at}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- unsafe code
# Stage 1's (proposal/22-unsafe.md; D-080 to D-085): the three unsafe
# operations outside an `unsafe:` block or an `unsafe` expression, the
# markers that oblige, and raw pointers.
E0981 error   unsafe.call                 `{name}` is an `unsafe def`: call it inside an `unsafe:` block or an `unsafe` expression
E0982 error   unsafe.foreign-call         `{name}` is a foreign function, whose signature is a claim the compiler cannot check: call it inside an `unsafe:` block or an `unsafe` expression
E0983 error   unsafe.fn-value             `{name}` is {what}, which cannot be a function value: a call through the value would need no `unsafe`
E0984 error   unsafe.impl-needed          `{trait}` is an `unsafe trait`, whose implementors keep a promise the compiler cannot check: its impl is written `unsafe impl`
E0985 error   unsafe.impl-promises-nothing  {what}, so `unsafe impl` promises nothing: write `impl`
E0986 error   unsafe.never-asserted       `{trait}` is never asserted: the compiler decides it from a type's parts, and nothing may claim it otherwise
E0987 error   unsafe.not-ptr              `ptr mut T` is the raw pointer of `std.mem`, and `{name}` here names {found}
E0988 error   unsafe.in-contract          a contract holds no `unsafe`: it may call safe functions built on unsafe code, and unsafe code relies on no contract
-     label   unsafe.its-obligation       its obligation: {obligations}
-     label   unsafe.its-obligations      its obligations: {obligations}
-     help    unsafe.write-reason         write why they hold in a `# safety:` comment above the block, naming each obligation it discharges
-     help    unsafe.call-in-closure      call it from a closure, whose `unsafe` expression says why its obligations hold: `(...) -> unsafe {name}(...)`
-     fix     unsafe.write-unsafe-impl    write `unsafe impl`
-     fix     unsafe.remove-unsafe        remove `unsafe`
-     help    unsafe.import-ptr           `ptr[T]` and `ptr mut T` are the raw pointers of `std.mem`: `from std.mem import ptr`
-     help    unsafe.take-capability      take the capability whose authority the call exercises (22, clause 5), as `std.rt.os` does behind `System`
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R19; 22, "Foreign calls"): exported functions and
# blocking foreign calls.
E0989 error   unsafe.export-signature     `{name}` is `@export`ed, and foreign code that calls it cannot keep `{ty}` alive: an exported function's signature holds no reference, view, `Shared`, closure or capability
E0990 error   unsafe.blocking-not-foreign  `@blocking` marks a foreign function whose call may block its thread, and `{name}` is not a foreign function
# Stage 1's (RULES.md, R118; 12, "C interop"): a type C has no layout for
# at the boundary.
E0991 error   unsafe.export-not-c         `{name}` is `@export`ed, and `{ty}` has no layout C shares: an exported function takes and returns numbers, `bool`, `char`, raw pointers and types declared `@repr(c)`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- packages and manifests
E1000 error   package.toml-syntax         this is not TOML that `sisu.toml` reads: expected {expected}, found {found}
E1001 error   package.toml-duplicate      `{key}` is given more than once in `{table}`
E1002 error   package.missing-key         `{table}` needs `{key}`
E1003 error   package.bad-value           `{key}` takes {expected}, found {found}
E1004 error   package.bad-name            `{name}` cannot name a package: {why}
E1005 error   package.no-manifest         the dependency `{name}` has no `sisu.toml` at `{path}`
E1006 error   package.renamed-dependency  the dependency `{name}` is the package `{found}`; a path dependency is named by its own `[package] name`
E1007 error   package.cycle               the packages depend on each other in a cycle: {cycle}; packages build in dependency order, so their graph has no cycle
E1008 error   package.no-root-module      the package `{name}` has no root module; add `src/main.sisu` (an application) or `src/lib.sisu` (a library)
E1009 error   package.two-root-modules    the package `{name}` has both `src/main.sisu` and `src/lib.sisu`; a package has one root module
E1010 error   package.not-a-path          the dependency `{name}` is not a `path` dependency; stage 0 has no registry, so a dependency is `{name} = {{ path = "../{name}" }}`
E1011 error   package.bad-when            `when` takes the build target's facts `os`, `arch` and `runtime`, each a string or a list of strings, as in `when = {{ os = "windows" }}`
E1012 error   package.module-name         `{file}` is not a module: `{name}` is not a module name; a module is named in lowercase letters, digits and `_`
E1013 error   package.no-member           the workspace member `{path}` has no `sisu.toml`
E1014 error   package.script-metadata     a script's metadata starts with `# /// script` and ends with a line `# ///`; this block has no end
E1015 error   package.trust-unknown       `[trust] unsafe` names `{name}`, which is not a dependency of this package
E1016 error   package.no-package          `{path}` has `[workspace]` but no `[package]`, so it cannot be a dependency
E1017 error   package.reserved-name       `{name}` is the name of a standard-library module, which `import {name}` names; a package of the program needs another name
