# Hot replacement (`sisu run --watch`; 10, "Debugging and profiling";
# D-053, D-068): what a save did to the running program.
R0109 runtime run.live-epoch              watch: epoch {epoch} replaces {functions}
R0110 runtime run.live-rejected           watch: the save of {files} does not check, so the program keeps running epoch {epoch}:\n{diagnostics}
R0111 runtime run.live-restart-signature  watch: the signature of `{function}` changed, so the program starts again
R0112 runtime run.live-restart-layout     watch: the layout of `{ty}` changed, so the program starts again
R0114 runtime run.live-restart-impls      watch: the impls of `{trait}` changed, so the program starts again
R0115 runtime panic.char-boundary         the byte index {index} is inside a character of the text
R0116 runtime run.stats-depth             stats stack-depth: {calls} calls, the deepest stack {max} frames, about {bytes} bytes of native stack there (16 per SIR local and 64 per frame)
R0117 runtime run.stats-depth-bucket      stats stack-depth: {calls} calls at a depth of {from} to {to} frames
R0118 runtime run.stats-shared-sites      stats shared: {local} of {total} `Shared` construction sites tagged task-local, {crossing} may-cross
R0119 runtime run.stats-shared-site       stats shared: {at} in `{function}`, {tag}: {cells} cells made, {crossed} reached by another task
R0120 runtime run.stats-tag-local         task-local
R0121 runtime run.stats-tag-crossing      may-cross
# A natively compiled program broke a rule its runtime relies on (stage 2;
# docs/architecture.md, "Stage 2").
R0122 runtime run.native-internal         internal error in the native runtime: {detail}
# `--stats` of a native run: the latency in microseconds (native code counts
# no statements).
R0124 runtime run.stats-latency-native    stats: scheduling latency p50 {p50us} p99 {p99us} microseconds (native code counts no statements)
# A native run under `SISU_RT_ALLOCS` (stage 2, decision 217): the runtime's
# allocations of values still live when the program ends.
R0125 runtime run.live-allocations        allocations: {count} of the runtime's live at exit
# A native run under `SISU_RT_ALLOCS=peak` (decision 229): the most of the
# runtime's allocations of values that were live at once, before R0125.
R0126 runtime run.peak-allocations        allocations: at most {count} of the runtime's live at once
# A panic's stack trace deeper than its innermost and outermost frames
# shows (decision 234): the line that stands for the frames between.
R0127 runtime run.trace-elided            ... (frames left out)
# A generator dropped before its end is closed: its frames unwind, running
# their drops and `defer`s (decision 236). A panic there aborts, as one
# during unwinding does, and so does a wait.
R0128 runtime panic.during-abandon        panic while a generator dropped before its end cleans up: {message}\n  a panic there aborts the program
R0129 runtime panic.abandoned-wait        a generator dropped before its end cannot wait while it cleans up
# A generator that code which cannot wait takes items from (another
# iterator's adapter, an intrinsic's `Iterable[T]` argument) runs to its
# next `yield` there and then; a wait there aborts the program (decision
# 247).
R0130 runtime panic.driven-wait           a generator inside another iterator, or given to `sum`, `zip` and the other functions of an `Iterable[T]`, cannot wait; iterate it with `for`
# The checked memory model (D-085; 22, "Interaction with the rest of the
# language"; docs/architecture.md, "The checked memory model"): the
# interpreter's checks of raw memory, each naming the obligation of the
# `Safety:` list in std.mem that the operation broke. `{op}` is the
# operation (`ptr.read`, `RawBuf`'s `ptr.write`, a foreign function), and
# offsets and sizes are in bytes of the block the pointer points into.
R0131 runtime panic.raw-bounds            `{op}` of {bytes} bytes at offset {offset} of a block of {size} bytes reaches outside it (`{obligation}`)
R0132 runtime panic.raw-offset            `ptr.offset` by {count} from offset {offset} of a block of {size} bytes leaves it (`in_bounds`)
R0133 runtime panic.raw-misaligned        `{op}` of `{ty}` at offset {offset} of its block, which is not aligned to {align} (`aligned`)
R0134 runtime panic.raw-released          `{op}` of memory that was released (`{obligation}`)
R0135 runtime panic.raw-gone              `{op}` through a pointer to a value that no longer exists (`{obligation}`)
R0136 runtime panic.raw-uninitialised     `{op}` of `{ty}` at offset {offset} of a block of {size} bytes, which holds no value there (`{obligation}`)
R0137 runtime panic.raw-mistyped          `{op}` of `{ty}` at offset {offset}, where the memory holds a value of type `{held}` (`{obligation}`)
R0138 runtime panic.raw-invalid-bits      `{op}` gives the bits {bits}, which are not a valid value of type `{ty}` (`{obligation}`)
R0139 runtime panic.raw-shared-write      `{op}` through a pointer made from a shared borrow (`{obligation}`)
R0140 runtime panic.raw-overwrite         `{op}` over a value of type `{ty}` at offset {offset} that was never dropped (`writable`)
R0141 runtime panic.raw-no-memory         `{op}` through a pointer with no memory behind it (`{obligation}`)
R0142 runtime panic.raw-release-other     `{op}` of memory that `{allocator}` did not give out, or not at its start (`from_here`)
R0143 runtime panic.raw-release-twice     `{op}` of memory that was released already (`once`)
R0144 runtime panic.raw-release-layout    `Global.release` with a layout of {size} bytes aligned to {align} of a block acquired with {block_size} bytes aligned to {block_align} (`from_here`)
R0145 runtime panic.raw-release-live      `{op}` of memory that still holds a value of type `{ty}` at offset {offset}, which was never dropped: every value is dropped once (22, the soundness contract, clause 3)
R0146 runtime panic.raw-sizes             `{op}` from `{from}` of {from_size} bytes to `{to}` of {to_size} bytes: the sizes differ
R0147 runtime panic.raw-plain-short       `read_plain` of `{ty}` of {size} bytes at {at} of {len} bytes reaches past their end
R0148 runtime panic.raw-read-only         `{op}` of memory that foreign code gave out to be read only (`writable`)
# A foreign call the interpreter does not make (Q-55): it emulates a few
# functions of the C library, and runs no other foreign code.
R0149 runtime panic.foreign-unknown       the interpreter makes no foreign call: `{name}` is not one of the C functions it emulates ({known}); `sisu run --native` calls it
R0150 runtime panic.foreign-null          `{name}` returned a null pointer, which its declared result `{ty}` cannot hold
R0151 runtime panic.foreign-overlap       `memcpy` of {bytes} bytes from a range that overlaps the one it writes, which C's `memcpy` does not allow
R0152 runtime panic.foreign-fd            `write` to the file descriptor {fd}, which the interpreter does not emulate; it emulates 1 and 2, the standard streams
# A machine intrinsic of `std.rt` (22, "Machine intrinsics"; D-086): native
# code only, since the interpreter runs its tasks on its own scheduler.
R0153 runtime panic.machine-intrinsic     `{name}` is a machine intrinsic of native code; the interpreter runs its tasks on a scheduler of its own
R0154 runtime run.expect-failed          expectation failed: {expr}
R0155 runtime run.expect-failed-message  expectation failed: {expr}\n  message: {message}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- driver

D0001 cli     cli.cannot-read             cannot read `{path}`: {reason}
D0002 cli     cli.usage                   usage: sisu <command> [options] [<file>]\n\nWithout a file, a command works on the package of the current directory (`sisu.toml`).\n\ncommands:\n  run [<file>] [args...]  check and run a program (`--watch` swaps in each save, `--sir` runs a SIR file, `--monitor` checks ownership while it runs, `--defer-borrow` runs a program the ownership checker rejected under the monitor, `--contracts=off|preconditions|all` sets what it checks, `--record LOG` writes its capability log, `--stats` reports the scheduler's numbers, `--stats stack-depth` a histogram of the frame depths, `--stats shared` each `Shared` construction site, `--native` compiles it to an executable and runs that, `--release` with the release profile, `--from-sir FILE` runs a file of serialised SIR, natively with `--native`, and `--native --from-lir FILE` a file of LIR, stage 2's lowering of SIR)\n  check [<file>]         report diagnostics (`--format=ids` for the conformance form, `--diary LOG` appends them and what resolved the last check's to a JSONL log, `--holes <file>` lists each typed hole of the program with the type its place needs and the bindings in scope that fit it)\n  doc [<path>]           the documentation, a Markdown page per module, printed each after a line naming its file (`--output DIR` writes the pages into DIR instead, `--std [MODULE...]` documents the standard library, or the modules named, as `std.time`)\n  build [<file>]         check and lower a package, writing its lockfile (`--native` also compiles a program to an executable: a package's to `build/debug/NAME` beside its lockfile, `build/release/NAME` with `--release`, a file's beside it, named by its stem, or at `--output PATH`; `--native --from-sir` compiles a file of serialised SIR, `--native --from-lir` a file of LIR)\n  test [<file>]          run the tests and transcripts under the ownership monitor (`--no-monitor` without it, `--no-cache` runs the tests that passed before too, `--jobs N`, `--filter TEXT`, `--tag T`, `--skip-tag T`, `--update` for snapshots and transcripts, `--replay TOKEN`, `--sim` with `--faults P`, `--std` for the standard library's doc examples, `--from-sir FILE` runs the tests of a file `sisu sir --tests` printed, `--native` compiles them and runs each natively, `--release` with the release profile)\n  bench [<file>]         run the benchmarks (`--iterations N`, `--samples K` times each after a run not timed, `--from-sir FILE` those of a file `sisu sir --tests` printed)\n  replay <log>           run a recorded program again against its capability log\n  fmt [<path>...]        format files in place (`--check` reports what would change)\n  repl [<file>|<dir>]    an interactive session, with a package loaded from its directory (`--echo` writes each input after its prompt, for a session read from a pipe)\n  ws <file>              run a worksheet, showing each statement's value (`--run` with the real `System`)\n  lsp                    the language server, over standard input and output\n  new <name>             create a package (`--lib` for a library)\n  sir [<file>]           print the serialised SIR (`--tests`: of the program built with its tests and benchmarks)\n  tokens <file>          print the token stream (`--trivia` includes trivia)\n  parse <file>           `--tree` prints the syntax tree, `--ast` the AST built from it, `--print` reprints the source, `--shape` prints what formatting keeps\n  explain <code>         describe a diagnostic code\n  explain-borrow <file>:<line>  show every borrow live on a line\n  audit --ownership <file>...  count the uses of `Shared`, `Weak`, `Cell`, `Arena`, `Key`, `.copy()` and `unsafe` in each file\n  audit --caps [<file>...]  the capability parameters of each function that does I/O, the depth from `main` of each use of a capability, and the structs bundling three or more (without a file, of the package's program)\n  audit --unsafe [--std] [<path>]  every `unsafe` block and function of a program by module, with its `# safety:` reason, and its package's level\n  lint [<path>]          the lints beyond the compiler's warnings, with their fixes\n  fix <file>             apply the preferred fix of each diagnostic of the file, checking it again until none applies (`--dry-run` prints the edits and leaves the file, `--all` fixes the lints too), from stage 1\n  eval <file>            the constants of a configuration module, as JSON (`--to toml` or `--to yaml` for TOML or YAML), from stage 1's compile-time evaluator\n\n`run`, `check`, `build`, `doc`, `test` and `bench` take `--locked`, which keeps `sisu.lock` as it is, and `--features NAMES` (the package's features to turn on, separated by commas, `DEP/FEATURE` for a dependency's) and `--no-default-features`. `run` and `test` take `--schedule-seed N`, `--explore N` (N seeds), `--scheduler random|pct|exhaustive` and `--depth N`.\n\nThe front end is stage 1's, the one written in Sisu, for `check`, `sir`, `build`, `run`, `test`, `bench`, `fmt`, `doc`, `tokens`, `parse` (`--shape` too), `repl`, `ws` and `eval`, which only it has; `--front-end=rust` before a command's file, or `SISU_FRONT_END=rust` for a whole process, runs the Rust front end, the oracle, instead.
